//! lotus/stereo_top_tail.rye — ALES150: the top-and-tail trimmer carried into stereo, ONE BRACKET for BOTH channels — cut only the leading and trailing JOINT
//! silence, keep every interior pause on both channels in lockstep. ALES98 named `top_tail`: segment a mono clip through ALES95, find the first and last non-silent
//! runs, then cut ONLY the leading and trailing silence, leaving every internal pause where the speaker left it — the single most-used silence gesture in every
//! recorder and DAW, between ALES96's full strip and ALES97's split. Carried into stereo it meets exactly the wall ALES148 and ALES149 met: a Lotus master is a
//! StereoClip (ALES10), two Clips whose defining invariant is that LEFT and RIGHT hold the SAME length, and ALES147 proved the two channels segment INDEPENDENTLY —
//! their silences differ in count and boundary. Top-and-tailing each channel by its OWN silence would give the two speakers DIFFERENT lengths and tear the stereo
//! image apart. So this rung cannot run the mono trimmer twice; it must decide ONE bracket that serves BOTH.
//!
//! The rule, stated once — the SILENCE INTERSECTION at the ends: segment BOTH channels through ALES147 over one shared frame grid, mark a frame jointly-silent only
//! where it is `.silent` in BOTH channels, then keep the span from the FIRST frame either speaker fills to the LAST frame either speaker fills, cutting the leading
//! and trailing jointly-silent runs from BOTH channels in lockstep. A frame silent in only ONE channel is content the other speaker carries, so it bounds the kept
//! span; a moment where NEITHER speaker carries sound at the two ends is dead air, and only that dead air is removed. Every INTERIOR pause — jointly-silent or not —
//! is kept exactly where it lies. Both channels are cut at identical positions, so they leave exactly as long as each other: the stereo image stays whole.
//!
//! The crux: this rung invents no new measurement and no new sample. It reads its silences through ALES147 (which reads them through ALES95, ALES94, and the two
//! readers those fuse) and removes whole frames through ALES2's own `cut`; it fabricates nothing and reorders nothing. What is NEW is the composition rule — the
//! outer bracket of two independent silences — and its whole honesty is that running the proven mono `top_tail` (ALES98) on each channel apart would DESYNC the pair,
//! where this brackets once for both and keeps them balanced. The tail is cut FIRST so the leading index stays valid, exactly as the mono trimmer taught.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES150.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-053823_fill-ales150-lotus-stereo-top-tail.md
//! Stands on: lotus/stereo_segment.rye (ALES147) — the stereo voice-activity segmenter, run once to read BOTH channels' silences over one shared frame grid, its
//! `segment_stereo`, its `StereoSegments`, and its `StereoSegmentError` reused whole; lotus/segment.rye (ALES95) — the mono segmenter (the frame grid and `.silent`
//! verdict, through ALES147) and `max_segments`; lotus/trim_silence.rye (ALES96) — the mono silence stripper, through the stereo trimmer below the length bracket;
//! lotus/stereo_trim_silence.rye (ALES148) — the stereo silence stripper, imported by the selftest to prove the length-bracket law; lotus/stereo_split_silence.rye
//! (ALES149) — the stereo auto-splitter, imported by the selftest to prove the outer-take bracket law; lotus/top_tail.rye (ALES98) — the mono top-and-tail, imported
//! by the selftest to prove the LOCKSTEP law (that running it per channel would desync the pair); lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the StereoClip and its balance invariant;
//! lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, its `cut`, its `max_clip`, and the EditError reused whole; lotus/voiced.rye (ALES94) — the `.silent` verdict this intersects.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. This rung WRITES — it edits both clips —
//! yet only by removing whole jointly-silent frames at the two ends through ALES2's own cut, on both channels in lockstep; it fabricates no samples and reorders
//! nothing, and it never touches a sample between the first and last not-jointly-silent frames. NOT a transcript, not a real adaptive VAD with lookahead; those stay
//! later rungs. No real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_top_tail.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const segment = @import("segment.rye");
const stereo_segment = @import("stereo_segment.rye");
const stereo_trim_silence = @import("stereo_trim_silence.rye");
const stereo_split_silence = @import("stereo_split_silence.rye");
const top_tail = @import("top_tail.rye");

pub const stereo_top_tail_version = "20260815.053823";

/// The stereo top-and-tail's faults are the stereo segmenter's OWN faults (ALES147's StereoSegmentError, itself ALES95's SegmentError: the classifier's band/floor
/// faults, plus BadFrame and the honest unreachable SegmentsFull) and the editor's OWN faults (ALES2's EditError from cut), each reused by name. The stereo lift
/// invents none of its own — an empty clip refuses BadFrame through the segmenter (a zero span cannot carry a legal frame), so a keeper reads one error vocabulary
/// across the read and the two end cuts, mono or stereo.
pub const StereoTopTailError = stereo_segment.StereoSegmentError || timeline.EditError;

/// Trim ONLY the leading and trailing JOINT silence from both channels of `sc`, leaving every interior pause exactly where it lies. Segments BOTH channels of the
/// whole clip `[0, len)` into frames of `frame_len` samples through ALES147, marks a frame jointly-silent only where it is `.silent` in BOTH channels, finds the FIRST
/// and LAST frame that is NOT jointly-silent, then cuts the trailing joint-silence and the leading joint-silence through ALES2's `cut` — tail first, so the leading
/// index stays valid — from BOTH channels in lockstep. A frame either speaker fills bounds the kept span; an interior jointly-silent frame is KEPT. Validates the
/// whole span, band, floor, and frame length through ALES147's own up-front checks BEFORE any cut; on refusal both channels are untouched. An empty clip refuses
/// `BadFrame`. An all-jointly-silent master trims to empty on both; a no-joint-silent master is unchanged on both.
pub fn stereo_top_and_tail(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    frame_len: u32,
    t_low: i32,
    t_high: i32,
    silence_floor: u32,
    voice_split: u32,
) StereoTopTailError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced — the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the whole edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    const original_len: u32 = sc.left.len;

    // Segment BOTH channels once over one shared frame grid. ALES147 runs all of its (and ALES95's) validation here — band/span precheck, the floor ceiling, the
    // dividing frame length, the empty-clip BadFrame — read-only, before a single sample is cut. On refusal it leaves the source untouched, so both channels are too.
    var segs = stereo_segment.StereoSegments{};
    try stereo_segment.segment_stereo(sc, &segs, 0, original_len, frame_len, t_low, t_high, silence_floor, voice_split);

    // invariant: segmenting a non-empty clip yields at least one run per channel that tiles it — ALES147's own postcondition, relied on here.
    assert(segs.left.len >= 1 and segs.left.len <= segment.max_segments);
    assert(segs.right.len >= 1 and segs.right.len <= segment.max_segments);

    // The frames tile [0, original_len) exactly — the segmenter refused a non-dividing or zero frame length, so this division is exact and at least one.
    const frames: u32 = original_len / frame_len;
    // invariant: the frame count is real and at least one.
    assert(frames >= 1);

    // Forward pass — the SILENCE INTERSECTION at the ends. Walk the frames left to right, advancing one cursor per channel across its own tiling run list, and mark a
    // frame jointly-silent ONLY where it is `.silent` in both channels. Record the FIRST and LAST frame that is NOT jointly-silent — the bracket either speaker fills.
    // `found` stays false only when every frame is jointly silent (the whole master is dead air).
    var found: bool = false;
    var first_ns: u32 = 0;
    var last_ns: u32 = 0;
    var li: u32 = 0;
    var ri: u32 = 0;
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < frames) : (k += 1) {
        const fs: u32 = k * frame_len;
        // Runs tile [0, original_len) in order and frame starts only increase, so each cursor advances monotonically to the run covering this frame's start.
        while (li + 1 < segs.left.len and segs.left.items[li].start + segs.left.items[li].count <= fs) : (li += 1) {}
        while (ri + 1 < segs.right.len and segs.right.items[ri].start + segs.right.items[ri].count <= fs) : (ri += 1) {}
        // invariant: each cursor names the run that actually covers fs — the runs tile the span, so exactly one always does.
        assert(segs.left.items[li].start <= fs and fs < segs.left.items[li].start + segs.left.items[li].count);
        assert(segs.right.items[ri].start <= fs and fs < segs.right.items[ri].start + segs.right.items[ri].count);
        const joint_silent: bool = segs.left.items[li].verdict == .silent and segs.right.items[ri].verdict == .silent;
        if (!joint_silent) {
            if (!found) {
                first_ns = k;
                found = true;
            }
            last_ns = k;
        }
    }

    if (!found) {
        // ALL JOINTLY SILENT: no living span to keep — the whole master is leading-and-trailing dead air, so top-and-tail removes it all from both channels.
        // invariant: cutting the whole span leaves each channel empty; the span is legal since it is exactly the clip, and the two cuts are identical, so balance holds.
        try timeline.cut(&sc.left, 0, original_len);
        try timeline.cut(&sc.right, 0, original_len);
        // invariant: both channels are empty when this runs, so the trim is proven to have
        // taken everything rather than left a head or a tail behind
        assert(sc.left.len == 0 and sc.right.len == 0);
        return;
    }

    // The kept span runs from the first not-jointly-silent frame to the last, interior joint-silence included. The frames tile [0, original_len), so these are exact
    // sample boundaries.
    const kept_start: u32 = first_ns * frame_len; // samples before the first not-joint frame — the leading joint-silence
    const kept_end: u32 = (last_ns + 1) * frame_len; // one past the last not-joint frame
    // invariant: the kept span sits inside the clip and is non-empty (a not-joint frame exists), so lead <= kept_end <= original.
    assert(kept_start <= kept_end and kept_end <= original_len);

    // Cut the TRAILING joint-silence first (from kept_end to the clip's end) from BOTH channels. A suffix cut shifts nothing before it, so the leading index stays
    // valid for the next cut; cutting the SAME span from both keeps them balanced.
    const tail_count: u32 = original_len - kept_end;
    if (tail_count > 0) {
        // invariant: the tail span lies wholly at each clip's end.
        assert(kept_end + tail_count == sc.left.len and kept_end + tail_count == sc.right.len);
        try timeline.cut(&sc.left, kept_end, tail_count);
        try timeline.cut(&sc.right, kept_end, tail_count);
    }

    // Cut the LEADING joint-silence (from the start, `kept_start` samples) from BOTH channels. Everything after it — the kept span — shifts down to the front, every
    // interior pause intact; the same span cut from both keeps them balanced.
    if (kept_start > 0) {
        // invariant: the leading span still lies at the front of each channel — the tail cut touched only samples after it.
        assert(kept_start <= sc.left.len and kept_start <= sc.right.len);
        try timeline.cut(&sc.left, 0, kept_start);
        try timeline.cut(&sc.right, 0, kept_start);
    }

    // invariant: both channels hold exactly the living span — the last not-joint frame's end minus the first's start — and leave balanced.
    assert(sc.left.len == kept_end - kept_start);
    assert(sc.right.len == kept_end - kept_start);
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO SPAN LAW — stereo_top_and_tail equals a by-hand keep of the original over [first_not_joint.start, last_not_joint.end) per channel, across
//     many frame lengths, floors, and splits, both channels balanced (the crux); THE BRACKET LAW — it equals the original bracketed by ALES149's stereo split outer
//     takes byte for byte, and stereo_trim_silence.len <= stereo_top_tail.len <= original.len; THE PRESERVATION / LOCKSTEP LAW — an interior jointly-silent frame is
//     KEPT (strictly longer than the joint trim on a gapped master), where running the mono top_tail per channel would DESYNC the pair; THE DEGENERATE / ATOMICITY
//     LAW — an all-joint-silent master trims to empty on both, a no-joint-silent master unchanged on both, head-only and tail-only cases, and a shared BadFrame /
//     BadThreshold / BadFloor refuses before any cut, both channels untouched and balanced -----------------------------------------------------------------------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

/// The verdict of the frame that starts at sample `fs`, read from a mono segmentation by finding the run that covers it — an independent reference to the cursor walk
/// inside `stereo_top_and_tail`, so the crux is proven against a second implementation.
fn frame_verdict(segs: *const segment.Segments, fs: u32) segment.Segment {
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < segs.len) : (i += 1) {
        if (segs.items[i].start <= fs and fs < segs.items[i].start + segs.items[i].count) return segs.items[i];
    }
    unreachable; // the runs tile the span, so a frame start always lands in one
}

/// The hand-built expectation the stereo top-and-tail is checked against in the span law — segment BOTH channels of the ORIGINAL, find the first and last frame NOT
/// jointly-silent, and copy the original's samples over [first.start, last.end) on each channel into `out`. Kept independent of stereo_top_and_tail's own loop so the
/// crux compares two paths, not one against itself. An all-jointly-silent master leaves `out` empty.
fn by_hand(out: *pan.StereoClip, src: *const pan.StereoClip, frame_len: u32, t_low: i32, t_high: i32, floor: u32, split: u32) !void {
    var ls = segment.Segments{};
    try segment.segment(&src.left, &ls, 0, src.left.len, frame_len, t_low, t_high, floor, split);
    var rs = segment.Segments{};
    try segment.segment(&src.right, &rs, 0, src.right.len, frame_len, t_low, t_high, floor, split);
    const frames: u32 = src.left.len / frame_len;
    var found: bool = false;
    var first_ns: u32 = 0;
    var last_ns: u32 = 0;
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < frames) : (k += 1) {
        const fs: u32 = k * frame_len;
        const l_silent = frame_verdict(&ls, fs).verdict == .silent;
        const r_silent = frame_verdict(&rs, fs).verdict == .silent;
        if (!(l_silent and r_silent)) {
            if (!found) {
                first_ns = k;
                found = true;
            }
            last_ns = k;
        }
    }
    if (!found) return; // all jointly silent — nothing kept
    const start = first_ns * frame_len;
    const end = (last_ns + 1) * frame_len;
    try timeline.splice(&out.left, 0, src.left.samples()[start..end]);
    try timeline.splice(&out.right, 0, src.right.samples()[start..end]);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const band_low: i32 = -1000;
    const band_high: i32 = 1000;

    // Six frames of four samples, the two channels carrying DIFFERENT silences on purpose. Joint-silent iff BOTH channels are quiet in the frame:
    //   frame 0 — quiet BOTH (leading dead air, jointly silent)
    //   frame 1 — LEFT loud, right quiet (not joint — the FIRST kept frame)
    //   frame 2 — quiet BOTH (interior gap, jointly silent — KEPT by top-tail)
    //   frame 3 — LEFT loud, right quiet (not joint)
    //   frame 4 — left quiet, RIGHT loud (not joint — the LAST kept frame)
    //   frame 5 — quiet BOTH (trailing dead air, jointly silent)
    // Kept span [4, 20): lead 4, tail 4, length 16 on both.
    const left_sig = [_]i16{
        40,    -40,    40,     -40, // frame 0 — quiet
        20000, 20000,  -20000, -20000, // frame 1 — loud
        30,    -30,    30,     -30, // frame 2 — quiet (interior gap)
        20000, 20000,  -20000, -20000, // frame 3 — loud
        45,    -45,    45,     -45, // frame 4 — quiet
        50,    -50,    50,     -50, // frame 5 — quiet
    };
    const right_sig = [_]i16{
        41,    -41,    41,     -41, // frame 0 — quiet
        42,    -42,    42,     -42, // frame 1 — quiet (left loud keeps the frame)
        31,    -31,    31,     -31, // frame 2 — quiet (interior gap)
        43,    -43,    43,     -43, // frame 3 — quiet (left loud keeps the frame)
        20000, 20000,  -20000, -20000, // frame 4 — loud (keeps the frame)
        51,    -51,    51,     -51, // frame 5 — quiet
    };

    // 1. THE STEREO SPAN LAW — over many frame lengths, floors, and splits, stereo_top_and_tail equals the hand-built span from the first not-joint frame to the last,
    //    per channel, read from the original, interior joint-silence kept, both channels balanced.
    {
        const flens = [_]u32{ 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24 }; // each divides 24
        const floors = [_]u32{ 100, 500, 1000, 5000 }; // above the quiet ~40 rms, below the loud
        const splits = [_]u32{ 1, 2, 4 };
        var lci: u32 = 0;
        while (lci < flens.len) : (lci += 1) {
            var fi: u32 = 0;
            while (fi < floors.len) : (fi += 1) {
                var si: u32 = 0;
                while (si < splits.len) : (si += 1) {
                    var got = pan.StereoClip{};
                    try load_stereo(&got, &left_sig, &right_sig);
                    var src = pan.StereoClip{};
                    try load_stereo(&src, &left_sig, &right_sig);
                    var want = pan.StereoClip{};
                    try stereo_top_and_tail(&got, flens[lci], band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si]);
                    try by_hand(&want, &src, flens[lci], band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si]);
                    if (!eq(got.left.samples(), want.left.samples())) return error.LeftNotHandTrimmed;
                    if (!eq(got.right.samples(), want.right.samples())) return error.RightNotHandTrimmed;
                    if (got.left.len != got.right.len) return error.TopTailUnbalanced;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // 2. THE PRESERVATION LAW — with frame 4 and floor 500, only the LEADING (frame 0) and TRAILING (frame 5) joint-silence is removed; the interior joint-silent
    //    frame 2 is KEPT, and the frames either speaker fills (1, 3, 4) survive on both channels. Both leave 16 samples, the kept span [4, 20).
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &left_sig, &right_sig);
        try stereo_top_and_tail(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2);
        const want_left = [_]i16{
            20000, 20000,  -20000, -20000, // frame 1 — loud
            30,    -30,    30,     -30, // frame 2 — the interior gap, KEPT
            20000, 20000,  -20000, -20000, // frame 3 — loud
            45,    -45,    45,     -45, // frame 4 — quiet on the left, kept by the right
        };
        const want_right = [_]i16{
            42,    -42,    42,     -42, // frame 1 — quiet on the right, kept by the left
            31,    -31,    31,     -31, // frame 2 — the interior gap, KEPT
            43,    -43,    43,     -43, // frame 3 — quiet on the right, kept by the left
            20000, 20000,  -20000, -20000, // frame 4 — loud
        };
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &want_left)) return error.PreservationLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &want_right)) return error.PreservationRightWrong;
        if (sc.left.len != 16 or sc.right.len != 16) return error.PreservationLength;
    }

    // 3. THE BRACKET LAW — stereo_top_and_tail equals the original bracketed by ALES149's stereo split outer takes byte for byte, and its length sits between ALES148's
    //    joint trim and the original (equal to the trim only when there is no interior joint-silence). Here the interior gap makes it STRICTLY longer than the trim.
    {
        const flens = [_]u32{ 1, 2, 4, 6, 12 };
        const floors = [_]u32{ 500, 1000 };
        const splits = [_]u32{ 1, 2 };
        var lci: u32 = 0;
        while (lci < flens.len) : (lci += 1) {
            var fi: u32 = 0;
            while (fi < floors.len) : (fi += 1) {
                var si: u32 = 0;
                while (si < splits.len) : (si += 1) {
                    // The stereo split's outer takes name the bracket.
                    var src = pan.StereoClip{};
                    try load_stereo(&src, &left_sig, &right_sig);
                    var takes = stereo_split_silence.StereoTakes{};
                    try stereo_split_silence.split_stereo_on_silence(&src, &takes, flens[lci], band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si]);

                    var got = pan.StereoClip{};
                    try load_stereo(&got, &left_sig, &right_sig);
                    try stereo_top_and_tail(&got, flens[lci], band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si]);

                    // The joint trim through ALES148 — the length lower bound.
                    var trimmed = pan.StereoClip{};
                    try load_stereo(&trimmed, &left_sig, &right_sig);
                    try stereo_trim_silence.stereo_trim_silence(&trimmed, flens[lci], band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si]);

                    if (takes.len == 0) {
                        // all jointly silent — top-tail empty, trim empty
                        if (got.left.len != 0 or got.right.len != 0 or trimmed.left.len != 0) return error.AllSilentBracket;
                    } else {
                        const bracket_start = takes.items[0].start;
                        const bracket_end = takes.items[takes.len - 1].start + takes.items[takes.len - 1].count;
                        if (!eq(got.left.samples(), left_sig[bracket_start..bracket_end])) return error.LeftNotBracketed;
                        if (!eq(got.right.samples(), right_sig[bracket_start..bracket_end])) return error.RightNotBracketed;
                        // stereo_trim_silence.len <= stereo_top_tail.len <= original.len
                        if (!(trimmed.left.len <= got.left.len and got.left.len <= left_sig.len)) return error.LengthBracketBroken;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // 4. THE INTERIOR PAUSE IS KEPT — on the mixed master (frame 4, floor 500) the interior jointly-silent gap survives, so the stereo top-tail is STRICTLY longer
    //    than the joint trim. Top-tail keeps [4, 20) = 16 samples; the joint trim removes frames 0, 2, 5 and keeps only the 12 not-jointly-silent samples.
    {
        var top = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&top, &left_sig, &right_sig);
        try stereo_top_and_tail(&top, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2);
        if (top.left.len != 16 or top.right.len != 16) return error.TopTailWrongLength;

        var trimmed = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&trimmed, &left_sig, &right_sig);
        try stereo_trim_silence.stereo_trim_silence(&trimmed, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2);
        if (trimmed.left.len != 12 or trimmed.right.len != 12) return error.JointTrimWrongLength;
        if (!(trimmed.left.len < top.left.len)) return error.NotStrictlyLonger; // 12 < 16
    }

    // 5. THE LOCKSTEP LAW — the reason this rung exists. Running the proven mono top_tail on each channel APART would DESYNC the pair: the left's outer non-silent
    //    frames are 1 and 3, so mono top_tail brackets [4, 16) → length 12; the right's only non-silent frame is 4, so mono top_tail brackets [16, 20) → length 4.
    //    The stereo top-tail instead brackets both by the UNION of the two speakers' outer content, [4, 20), holding both at 16, so the stereo image never tears.
    {
        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &left_sig);
        try top_tail.top_and_tail(&ml, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &right_sig);
        try top_tail.top_and_tail(&mr, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2);
        if (ml.len == mr.len) return error.MonoDidNotDesync; // the honest premise: per-channel mono top_tail DOES desync here
        if (ml.len != 12 or mr.len != 4) return error.MonoDesyncUnexpected;

        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &left_sig, &right_sig);
        try stereo_top_and_tail(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2);
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.StereoTornApart;
        if (sc.left.len != 16) return error.StereoWrongLength; // the union bracket, not either channel's alone
    }

    // 6. THE DEGENERATE LAW, all-joint-silent — a master quiet on BOTH channels everywhere trims to empty on both.
    {
        const quiet_l = [_]i16{ 40, -40, 40, -40, 30, -30, 30, -30 };
        const quiet_r = [_]i16{ 41, -41, 41, -41, 31, -31, 31, -31 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &quiet_l, &quiet_r);
        try stereo_top_and_tail(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2);
        if (sc.left.len != 0 or sc.right.len != 0) return error.AllJointSilentNotEmpty;
    }

    // 7. THE DEGENERATE LAW, no-joint-silent — a master loud on at least one channel in every frame is unchanged on both (no leading or trailing dead air). Here the
    //    right is loud throughout, so NOTHING is jointly silent even though the left goes quiet.
    {
        const some_l = [_]i16{
            40, -40, 40, -40, 20000, -20000, 20000, -20000, // quiet then loud on the left
        };
        const loud_r = [_]i16{
            20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, 20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // loud throughout on the right
        };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &some_l, &loud_r);
        try stereo_top_and_tail(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 1);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &some_l)) return error.NoJointSilentLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &loud_r)) return error.NoJointSilentRightChanged;
    }

    // 8. ONLY-LEADING and ONLY-TRAILING — a head-joint-silent master loses just its head; a tail-joint-silent master loses just its tail. Both channels in lockstep.
    {
        const head_l = [_]i16{
            40,    -40,    40,     -40, // frame 0 — quiet both (leading dead air)
            20000, 20000,  -20000, -20000, // frame 1 — loud
        };
        const head_r = [_]i16{
            41,    -41,    41,     -41, // frame 0 — quiet
            42,    -42,    42,     -42, // frame 1 — quiet (left loud keeps it)
        };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &head_l, &head_r);
        try stereo_top_and_tail(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2);
        const want_hl = [_]i16{ 20000, 20000, -20000, -20000 };
        const want_hr = [_]i16{ 42, -42, 42, -42 };
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &want_hl) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &want_hr)) return error.HeadNotTrimmed;
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.HeadUnbalanced;

        const tail_l = [_]i16{
            20000, 20000,  -20000, -20000, // frame 0 — loud
            40,    -40,    40,     -40, // frame 1 — quiet both (trailing dead air)
        };
        const tail_r = [_]i16{
            42,    -42,    42,     -42, // frame 0 — quiet (left loud keeps it)
            41,    -41,    41,     -41, // frame 1 — quiet
        };
        var sc2 = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc2, &tail_l, &tail_r);
        try stereo_top_and_tail(&sc2, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2);
        const want_tl = [_]i16{ 20000, 20000, -20000, -20000 };
        const want_tr = [_]i16{ 42, -42, 42, -42 };
        if (!eq(sc2.left.samples(), &want_tl) or !eq(sc2.right.samples(), &want_tr)) return error.TailNotTrimmed;
        if (sc2.left.len != sc2.right.len) return error.TailUnbalanced;
    }

    // 9a. AN EMPTY CLIP refuses BadFrame (nothing to trim), both channels untouched and balanced.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        if (stereo_top_and_tail(&sc, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2)) |_| return error.EmptySlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadFrame) return error.WrongEmpty;
        if (sc.left.len != 0 or sc.right.len != 0) return error.EmptyMutated;
    }

    // 9b. A RAGGED FRAME LENGTH — one that does not divide the clip — refuses BadFrame, both channels untouched.
    {
        const five_l = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
        const five_r = [_]i16{ 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &five_l, &five_r);
        if (stereo_top_and_tail(&sc, 2, band_low, band_high, 500, 2)) |_| return error.RaggedSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadFrame) return error.WrongRagged;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &five_l) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &five_r)) return error.RaggedMutated;
    }

    // 9c. AN INVERTED BAND refuses BadThreshold (through ALES147's precheck), both channels untouched.
    {
        const four_l = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40 };
        const four_r = [_]i16{ 50, 60, 70, 80 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &four_l, &four_r);
        if (stereo_top_and_tail(&sc, 2, 1000, -1000, 500, 2)) |_| return error.InvertedSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadThreshold) return error.WrongInverted;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &four_l) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &four_r)) return error.InvertedMutated;
    }

    // 9d. AN ILLEGAL FLOOR — above the peak ceiling — refuses BadFloor, both channels untouched and balanced.
    {
        const four_l = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40 };
        const four_r = [_]i16{ 50, 60, 70, 80 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &four_l, &four_r);
        if (stereo_top_and_tail(&sc, 2, band_low, band_high, 32769, 2)) |_| return error.BadFloorSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadFloor) return error.WrongBadFloor;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &four_l) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &four_r)) return error.BadFloorMutated;
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.BadFloorUnbalanced;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-top-tail: the TOP-AND-TAIL TRIMMER CARRIED INTO STEREO, one bracket for both channels — cut only the leading and trailing JOINT " ++
        "silence, keep every interior pause on both channels in lockstep. ALES98 top-and-tails a mono clip by cutting only its leading and trailing silence; carried " ++
        "into stereo it meets the wall the length-changing edits do — a StereoClip's defining invariant is that left and right hold the SAME length, yet ALES147 " ++
        "proved the two channels segment INDEPENDENTLY, their silences differing in count and boundary. Top-and-tailing each channel by its own silence would give " ++
        "the two speakers different lengths and tear the stereo image apart, so this rung cannot run the mono trimmer twice; it must decide ONE bracket for BOTH. " ++
        "The rule is the SILENCE INTERSECTION at the ends: segment both channels through ALES147 over one shared frame grid, mark a frame jointly-silent only where " ++
        "it is silent in BOTH channels, keep the span from the FIRST frame either speaker fills to the LAST, and cut the leading and trailing jointly-silent runs " ++
        "from both channels in lockstep, tail first so the leading index stays valid. A frame silent in only one channel bounds the kept span — the other speaker's " ++
        "content holds it — and every INTERIOR pause, jointly-silent or not, is kept where it lies; only dead air at the two ends is removed. The crux: it invents " ++
        "no new measurement and no new sample — it reads its silences through ALES147 and removes whole frames through ALES2's cut, so it can never drift from the " ++
        "segmenter it composes; what is new is the composition rule, the outer bracket of two independent silences, whose whole honesty is that running the proven " ++
        "mono top_tail on each channel apart would DESYNC the pair (the left bracketing to length 12, the right to length 4) where this brackets once for both and " ++
        "holds them balanced at 16. Proven: the stereo span law (the result equals a by-hand keep of the original over the first-to-last not-jointly-silent frame " ++
        "span, per channel, across many frame lengths, floors, and splits, both channels balanced); the bracket law (it equals the original bracketed by ALES149's " ++
        "stereo split outer takes byte for byte, and stereo_trim_silence.len <= stereo_top_tail.len <= original.len); the preservation and lockstep laws (the " ++
        "interior jointly-silent frame is KEPT, strictly longer than the joint trim on a gapped master, where the mono top_tail per channel desyncs to 12 and 4 " ++
        "while the stereo top-tail holds both at 16); the degenerate laws (an all-jointly-silent master trims to empty on both, a master loud on at least one " ++
        "channel per frame unchanged on both, a head-joint-silent master losing only its head, a tail-joint-silent only its tail); and the atomicity laws (an empty " ++
        "clip, a ragged frame length, an inverted band, and an illegal floor each refuse by name — BadFrame, BadFrame, BadThreshold, BadFloor — both channels " ++
        "untouched and still balanced). Purely local — it edits only by removing whole jointly-silent frames at the two ends through cut on both channels in " ++
        "lockstep, fabricating no samples and reordering nothing; NOT a transcript or a real adaptive VAD; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no " ++
        "real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
